    "body": "A strict export that hits an unexpected tensor name hangs the job instead of failing it.\n\n`save_generator` raised from inside its loop:\n\n```python\nfor name, tensor in generator:\n    all_yielded_keys.add(name)\n    if name not in all_expected_keys:\n        if strict:\n            raise KeyError(...)\n```\n\nIts own comment twelve lines above says why that is not ours to do:\n\n> In a distributed environment, only rank 0 should write to disk. Other ranks must still exhaust the generator to participate in collectives.\n\nThose ranks are inside `for _ in generator: pass`. When rank 0 leaves the generator mid-stream, they stay blocked in the gather for the tensor rank 0 never asked for, until the process-group timeout. `save_hf_weights` is where a user reaches this, its default is `strict=True`, and its docstring says \"This method is collective and must be called by all ranks\".\n\nMeasured on `53681c1` with `state.py` loaded on its own, a two-key shard and a generator that records what it was asked for:\n\n```\nKeyError: \"Tensor 'surprise.weight' from generator not found in the original model structure. ...\"\nitems the generator was asked for: ['model.first', 'surprise.weight']\nfully drained: False\n```\n\n`model.second` is never pulled. With this change the same probe reports `['model.first', 'surprise.weight', 'model.second']` and raises the same `KeyError`.\n\n### The distributed path had it too\n\n`_save_generator_distributed` carried the same mid-loop raise, and there it is worse: every rank both produces and consumes the generator, so any rank can be the one that walks away.\n\nThat function already has the machinery for this, and says so. `local_export_error` is gathered across ranks and raised afterwards, under this comment:\n\n> Strict-mode check: ensure all expected tensors were written. Aggregate per-rank missing counts so all ranks raise consistently (avoids hangs on the trailing barriers).\n\nand the error it raises reads `\"Checkpoint export failed after all ranks drained the tensor generator: ...\"`, which the mid-loop raise made untrue. The duplicate-key branch in the same loop, eight lines down, already records into `local_export_error` and continues rather than raising. The unexpected-key branch now does the same.\n\n### Scope\n\nBoth paths keep writing nothing past the first offender, exactly as raising on it did, and both raise once the generator is dry. `strict=False` is untouched. Every caller that passes `strict=False` is untouched too, including the training checkpoint path in `checkpointing.py`, whose own comment makes the same point (\"Other ranks drain the streaming export generator so TP/PP/EP collectives complete\").\n\n### Testing\n\nTwo cases in `tests/unit_tests/models/test_hf_pretrained_state.py`, next to the existing `save_generator` tests and using the same `_write_safetensors_index` and `_mock_single_rank_distributed` helpers: one for the single-writer path, one for the distributed one. Both assert the generator was pulled dry and that the error still arrives. Both fail on `main` and pass here, and I checked two control cases (a complete shard still saves, `strict=False` still warns and saves) which pass either way. No `torch.distributed` process group is needed.\n\nI could not run the repository's suite: `tests/unit_tests/conftest.py` imports `megatron.core`, which is not installable here, and `megatron/bridge/__init__` pulls in the diffusion bridges, which need it too. So the runs above load `state.py` standalone with `importlib`, which is the real class and the real method, and I replicated the two new test bodies verbatim against it. `ruff` 0.9.9 check and format are clean on both files.\n\nSame reporter as #6410, different file, no overlap.\n",
